    "marks": 15,
    "model": "An EU regulation is binding and applies directly in every member state once it takes effect, so Ireland does not need national legislation for it to apply. An Irish business must comply with its rules directly, and Irish courts apply them. An EU directive is binding as to the result it sets, but Ireland chooses how to achieve that result. The Irish government must transpose the directive into Irish law by the deadline, usually through an Act or a statutory instrument. If it fails to do so, the Commission can start infringement proceedings. The key distinction is that a regulation applies automatically, while a directive needs national action before its requirements take effect.",
